# Build context for the shared image. The Dockerfile copies exactly three
# things — pyproject.toml, src/ and protocol/ — so everything else is context
# transfer with no build output, and research/ alone is over a megabyte of
# markdown.

# Secrets. The API key is supplied at run time via env_file, never baked in.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Version control and tooling metadata
.git
.gitignore
.dockerignore
.github
.vscode
.idea

# Python build and cache artefacts
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv*/
venv/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Not needed to build or run the image. `db/schema.sql` is mounted into the
# postgres container, not into this one; tests run on the host.
research/
tests/
db/
docs/

# The frontend. It builds from its own context (./ui) with its own Dockerfile
# and never enters the Python image, so `ui/` alone is enough — the two paths
# after it are spelled out anyway, because they are the ones that hurt if a
# later edit narrows this rule: a populated node_modules is tens of thousands
# of files and hundreds of megabytes of context transfer for a build that would
# not read one byte of it, and dist/ is build output masquerading as source.
ui/
ui/node_modules/
ui/dist/

Makefile
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.*.yml
Dockerfile

# Root-level markdown only — BLUEPRINT.md, INTERFACES.md, README.md. Docker
# matches a bare `*.md` against single path components, so this leaves nested
# files alone, and the re-include below makes that non-accidental: the
# approved clinical text must reach the image or the agent refuses to start.
*.md
!protocol/**
